    <h1>Raven-Flóki and the Discovery of Iceland</h1>
    <p class="lore-subtitle">Hrafna-Flóki Vilgerðarson — Navigation by Raven</p>
  </header>

  <section class="lore-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p>Hrafna-Flóki ("Raven-Flóki") Vilgerðarson earned his name, according to <em>Landnámabók</em>, because he released ravens at sea to find land. The third raven flew ahead without returning — Flóki followed it to Iceland, probably in the mid-9th century CE. The technique parallels land-finding bird use described in other medieval sailing traditions.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>The Source: Landnámabók</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The story of Raven-Flóki comes from <em>Landnámabók</em> ("The Book of Settlements"), a medieval Icelandic text that records the settlement of Iceland. The earliest surviving recensions (<em>Sturlubók</em> and <em>Hauksbók</em>) date to the 13th and early 14th centuries, though the work is thought to draw on older oral tradition going back to the 12th century or earlier.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The settlement of Iceland is generally dated by historians to c. 870–930 CE, making <em>Landnámabók</em> at minimum 300 years removed from the events it describes. This does not make it unreliable, but it must be read as a later literary-historical record rather than a contemporary account.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>The Story as Told in Landnámabók</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The passage (paraphrase following Hermann Pálsson and Paul Edwards, 1972 translation of the relevant section):</p>
    <blockquote class="lore-quote">
      <p>Flóki Vilgerðarson was a great Viking. He made ready to sail to seek Garðarshólmi [an earlier name for Iceland], which had been found by Garðar. He took three ravens with him as guide-birds. When he released the first raven, it flew back astern toward Norway. The second, when released, flew up and then came back to the ship. The third flew forward past the prow and did not return; Flóki steered after it and found the land.</p>
      <footer>— Paraphrase of <em>Landnámabók</em>, Sturlubók recension<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Flóki wintered in Iceland but had a difficult stay — he is said to have climbed a mountain, seen ice in the fjord, and given the country the name "Iceland" (<em>Ísland</em>). He returned to Norway and spoke poorly of the land, though he later went back and settled.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Navigation by Bird — Historical Context</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The use of land-finding birds is not unique to Norse tradition. The Old Testament (Genesis 8:7–12) describes Noah releasing a raven and then a dove from the ark, with the birds' return or non-return indicating the presence of land. The Norse and biblical traditions may reflect a shared practical technique, an independent development of the same idea, or simple literary parallel — scholars are divided.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens do have a strong homing tendency and are capable of ranging many kilometres from a fixed point. Their ability to detect land from altitude makes them plausible navigation aids — a bird released at sea would fly toward the nearest land if land were within range, or return to the ship if none was detectable.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>The Name Hrafna-Flóki</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Old Norse <em>hrafn</em> means "raven". <em>Hrafna-</em> is the genitive plural ("of ravens"). The name <em>Hrafna-Flóki</em> therefore means "Flóki of the ravens" or "Ravens' Flóki" — a kenning-style epithet earned, according to the saga, by his navigation method.<sup><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p>
    <p>Whether the name preceded the story (and the navigation legend was invented to explain it), or whether the story is historical and the name followed from it, is a question <em>Landnámabók</em> cannot answer. It is a common challenge in saga studies.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section lore-bridge">
    <h2>The Birds Behind the Story</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens range up to 80+ km from a base in a single day and have excellent spatial memory. At sea, with no landmark to home toward, a raven would likely return to the ship — the only fixed point. Near land, it would continue to the shore. This is precisely the behaviour the story describes.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <p><a href="/species/common-raven/" class="lore-species-link">Common Raven — species profile →</a></p>
  </section>

